e a dummy local buffer or allocate from global memory instead. Writing the batched kernel... I'm realizing that keeping GLOBAL=true and GLOBAL=false AIRs in separate batches actually works well—the false batch runs quickly at ~49ms with ~535 instances, while the true batch takes ~417ms with ~935 instances, and each gets its own kernel launch anyway. So I'll draft a plan to batch the coset-parallel kernel launches in Round 0, subdividing by memory budget to avoid the all-or-nothing constraint that plagued the previous approach.\n\nThe plan involves restoring the batched kernel infrastructure from the earlier commit (the GLOBAL=false kernels plus new GLOBAL=true variants with intermediates support), re-adding the FFI layer, and then orchestrating the sub-batching in Rust by classifying AIRs as batch-eligible or individual, grouping by GLOBAL value, subdividing each group by memory budget, building metadata arrays, and launching kernels before syncing the stream.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the metadata structures—I need to extend the existing batch metadata to support intermediates pointers for the GLOBAL=true case. But I'm also reconsidering the original task constraints: the description says to reuse existing batched kernels and not batch GLOBAL=true, yet my analysis suggests that won't meet the stated goals. I think the clearest path forward is to propose both GLOBAL=true and GLOBAL=false batching with explicit justification, and let the review process evaluate whether this scope is correct.\n\nLet me verify the memory requirements for GLOBAL=true batching. With 935 instances, most have a single x-block since the coset-parallel threshold isn't hit. The temp_sums_buffer size depends on max_num_cosets and skip_domain—roughly 128 elements per instance. I'm working through the x_blocks calculation to confirm whether the memory footprint is actually reasonable given the block dimensions. Looking at the memory budget calculation in the reverted commit, I realize it was u